\fs32 \cf2 The manuscript proposes the Many Clocks Interpretation (MCI), a single-world, 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $\\psi$
\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236 -ontic, nonlocal framework that reframes quantum measurement as a driven-dissipative phase-synchronization process. The fundamental degree of freedom is the internal chiral phase coupling of the Dirac equation. The author demonstrates that in an isolated system, this coupling produces conservative, unitary normal-mode precession with no attractor (protecting superpositions). When opened to a macroscopic environment (a bulk), tracing out the bath deforms this conservative flow into a gradient-like Adler/Kuramoto potential containing stable, bistable, or multistable attracting basins. Measurement is thus defined as a two-stage process: fast resonant capture followed by irreversible bulk equilibration to a faint, thermally biased macroscopic reference phase.
